name=fetch
category=network
description=download HTTP or authenticated HTTPS resources without overwriting existing files
usage=fetch [OPTIONS] URL...
usage=fetch [OPTIONS] --input-file=FILE
option=-o FILE, --output=FILE    write one URL to FILE; it must not exist
option=-q, --quiet    suppress normal lifecycle, progress, and success output
option=-i FILE, --input-file=FILE    read one URL per line from FILE
option=--timings    show detailed DNS, TCP, TLS, transfer, and filesystem timings
option=-h, --help    show this quick help
note=URLs without a scheme use http://. HTTPS uses TLS 1.3 with the system CA store, trusted UTC, and hostname verification; there is no HTTP fallback.
note=HTTP redirects may upgrade to HTTPS. HTTPS-to-HTTP redirects are rejected. Relative redirects, Content-Length, chunked, and connection-close responses are supported.
note=Multiple URLs are downloaded sequentially; automatic names gain .1, .2, and later suffixes on collision.
note=URL list files use one URL per line; blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Explicit output files are never overwritten.
note=Normal output reports the requested URL, one resolved IPv4 address, TCP connect, TLS authentication for HTTPS, HTTP response metadata, destination, and a completion summary.
note=In-place progress is used only when standard output is the interactive console and is wide enough. Redirected output receives no carriage-return progress updates.
